<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Liberation Movement (Ghana)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a>–<a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Liberation Movement was a political party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> in 1954 to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> interests against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwame-nkrumah">Kwame Nkrumah</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-peoples-party">Convention People's Party</a>. Backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asantehene">Asantehene</a> and led in parliament by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuffy">Kofi</a> Abrefa Busia, it demanded a federal constitution but lost the 1956 election, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> became a unitary state at independence in 1957.</p></div>
